## Project-specific context (mock3)

This project spans TWO sibling TypeScript codelines; the injected diff may touch either or both. Report file paths relative to the codeline they belong to:
- mocka (mock-a): fare rules. Source in src/fares.ts, tests in test/fares.test.ts. Exports fareFor, totalFor, the Rider type, and the UPPER_SNAKE constants BASE_FARE_CENTS / CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS. Fares are integer cents; ages are whole years.
- mockb (mock-b): schedule rendering. Source in src/schedule.ts, tests in test/schedule.test.ts. Exports formatStops, totalMinutes, and the Stop type.

Both codelines are ESM ("type": "module") and use vitest (imports from 'vitest', describe/it/expect). Verification commands per codeline: npm test (vitest run) and npm run build (tsc --noEmit). There is no linter configured — do not raise lint-style findings.

Ticket-driven defect classes to scrutinise (these are the exact bugs in scope):
- MOCK3-1 (fare boundary at age 65): the documented rule is "65 AND OVER pay the concession fare". A strict inequality such as `rider.age > 65` excludes a 65-year-old and is a blocker; the correct boundary is `>= 65`. Check that any fix also adds/adjusts a boundary test at exactly age 65 — the existing tests only cover age 70 ("well past the boundary"), so a fix without an age-65 test is a test-coverage-gap finding.
- MOCK3-2 (final stop omitted from schedule): a loop bounded by `i < stops.length - 1` in formatStops silently drops the last stop and is a blocker; the correct bound is `i < stops.length`. Note that `stops.length - 1` is legitimate in totalMinutes (indexing the last element) — do not flag correct last-element indexing, only loop bounds that skip the final element. Check for a test asserting the final stop appears in the rendered string.

Other traps in this stack:
- fareFor has early returns ordered pass → senior → child; a boundary change must not reorder these branches (pass holders always pay 0, regardless of age).
- Both codelines keep doc comments stating the business rule directly above the exported function; if a fix changes behaviour, the comment must still match the code — flag comment/code drift as a major finding.

